# SHIFT 6 items (Wednesday 11/04 1900 to Thursday 11/05 0700, night shift, ends with transfer to Med-Surg). Exec'd by author.py so it can use E and parts.
items6 = []

items6.append({'id': '6.1', 'type': 'vitals', 'points': 0.75, 'title': 'Vital Signs Through the Last Night',
               'where': 'Flowsheets > Vital Signs',
               'prompt': 'Set the clock to 1900 on Wednesday 11/04. Copy the vital signs at each time and circle every abnormal value. Then write two sentences below: (1) what changed in his breathing support and pain across the night, and (2) one thing the vital signs cannot tell you about the infection.',
               'times': ['2026-11-04 19:00', '2026-11-04 23:00', '2026-11-05 03:00', '2026-11-05 05:00'],
               'times_label': ['Wed 1900', 'Wed 2300', 'Thu 0300', 'Thu 0500'],
               'rowsLabels': ['Temperature', 'Heart rate', 'Blood pressure', 'Respiratory rate', 'SpO2 (%)', 'Pain (0-10)'],
               'codes': ['TEMP', 'HR', 'BP', 'RR', 'SPO2', 'PAIN'],
               'key': {'rules': 'Score 0.75 x (cells correct / 24), rounded to the nearest 0.25. The two sentences are worth 0.25 (item 6.1b): (1) oxygen was 2 L/min nasal cannula until it was weaned to room air at 0300 (SpO2 95-96% throughout); pain fell from 4/10 (1900) to 3/10 (2100 on) on oxycodone, gabapentin and acetaminophen; (2) vital signs cannot show what the wound looks like (purulent drainage, odor, spreading erythema), the white count and procalcitonin, or whether the blood cultures are clearing; a normal temperature can also be masked by scheduled acetaminophen. Chart values: T 99.0, 98.9, 98.7, 98.8; HR 89, 87, 84, 82; BP 110/63, 113/64, 114/66, 112/64; RR 20, 19, 19, 18; SpO2 95, 96, 96, 96; pain 4, 3, 3, 3. Everything is in the normal range except the pain score and the low-grade temperature of 99.0 (accept "no abnormal values" with pain circled). Allow +/- 2 for rates and +/- 0.2 F.'}})
items6.append({'id': '6.1b', 'type': 'box', 'points': 0.25, 'lines': 6, 'title': 'Two sentences for item 6.1',
               'prompt': '(1) What changed in his breathing support and pain across the night? (2) One thing the vital signs cannot tell you about the infection.', 'where': 'Flowsheets > Vital Signs',
               'key': {'elements': [E('Both sentences correct: (1) 2 L nasal cannula to room air at 0300 with SpO2 96% and pain 4 to 3/10; (2) vital signs do not show the wound, WBC/procalcitonin or the cultures (acetaminophen can mask a fever).', 0.25, 'Flowsheet')]}})

items6.append({'id': '6.2', 'type': 'box', 'points': 0.75, 'lines': 9, 'title': 'Soft Diet and the Correction Scale',
               'prompt': 'Marcus eats a soft diet with shakes and the insulin infusion is off. (a) Read the dinner intake in the 2000 flowsheet and the glucose values at 1800, 0000 and 0600; using the lispro correction scale order, how many units were due for each? (b) The last hydrocortisone dose was at 0230: how will that change his glucose, and why is the risk of low glucose still small on this scale? (c) He eats only about half of each tray: name two actions for his nutrition before Friday\'s surgery.',
               'where': 'Flowsheets > GI (Diet / Feeding); Lab Results (Glucose, point of care); Orders (insulin lispro correction scale, soft diet); Intake/Output',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Dinner: ate 50% of the soft tray. Glucose 149 (1800), 141 (0000), 141 (0600); the scale gives 2 units only for 151-200 (4 units for 201-250), so 0 units each time (none were given on the MAR).', 0.25, 'Flowsheet, labs, order'),
                   E('Stopping hydrocortisone (stress-dose steroids raise glucose) means glucose should drift DOWN over the next day; the risk of hypoglycemia is small because only a correction scale (no basal insulin) is ordered and glucose is 141; recheck before meals/bedtime and treat under 70 per protocol; ask the provider whether to hold the scale when NPO after midnight Thursday.', 0.25, 'Orders, MAR'),
                   E('Any two: calorie count and record the percent of each meal; offer the high-protein shakes TID (ordered); help him sit upright to eat (aspiration precautions, 90 degrees); small frequent snacks; dietitian follow-up for higher calories/protein for wound healing and the donor site; vitamin C and zinc per MAR.', 0.25, 'Orders, dietitian note')]}})

items6.append({'id': '6.3', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.25, 'lines': 13, 'title': 'Pain and Sleep Now That He Is Awake',
               'prompt': 'Open the 2230 and 0315 nursing notes, the pain flowsheet and the MAR. (a) Which pain scale do you use now, and why is the CPOT no longer the right tool? Give his scores at rest and with turning or the soaks. (b) List the doses of oxycodone, gabapentin and acetaminophen he received overnight with times. At 0130 mafenide soaks hurt (5/10) and the oxycodone was given at 0215: what would you do differently for the next soak at 0930? (c) At 0615 you are about to give the acetaminophen tablet: what must you check on the MAR and the order before giving it, and what is his daily limit? (d) Name two ways you protected his sleep and one safety check you make after giving oxycodone with gabapentin.',
               'where': 'Chart Review > Notes (Nursing Note 2230, 0315); Flowsheets > Pain; MAR; Orders (acetaminophen, oxycodone, gabapentin)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Numeric 0-10 self-report scale, because he is awake (RASS 0), oriented and able to report; CPOT is for patients who cannot self-report (intubated or sedated). Scores: 4/10 at 1900, 3/10 at rest from 2100, 5/10 with turning and the soak (up to 6/10 with care).', 0.25, 'Flowsheet, note'),
                   E('Oxycodone 5 mg at 2100, 0215 and 0615; gabapentin 300 mg at 2200 and 0620; acetaminophen 1 g IV at 2200, then 1,000 mg tablet at 0615. Next time give the oxycodone 30-45 minutes BEFORE the 0930 soak (Dr. Okafor\'s plan), not after the pain starts.', 0.5, 'MAR, Okafor note'),
                   E('Check that no other acetaminophen is due or already given: the IV order ended at 0500 and the tablet order starts at 0600 (give only ONE form); last dose 2200 (more than 8 h ago); total in the last 24 h is 3 g (1400, 2200, 0615) and the limit on his order is 3 g/day because AST is 78, so the next dose is not before 1400.', 0.25, 'Orders, MAR, labs'),
                   E('Any two sleep measures: cluster care and turns, lights dimmed, door closed, noise reduced, earplugs offered, pre-medicate before turns. After oxycodone plus gabapentin: check RASS (hold for RASS -2 or lower), respiratory rate and SpO2, and reassess pain 30-60 minutes later.', 0.25, 'Note 2230, pharmacy')]}})

items6.append({'id': '6.4', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.25, 'lines': 13, 'title': 'Delirium and Alcohol Withdrawal Watch',
               'prompt': 'His drinking history and an ethanol level of 42 mg/dL at 0240 on Monday were charted. Sedation is off, so days 2 to 4 after the last drink are the highest-risk window. (a) Name the four features of the CAM-ICU and say what makes it positive; what are his CAM-ICU and RASS results on this shift? (b) List four signs of alcohol withdrawal or delirium tremens you would look for and which vital signs would change first. (c) Three non-drug actions that prevent delirium tonight. (d) If he became agitated and tremulous at 0400, what would you do first and what would you ask the provider (look at what he was told to avoid)?',
               'where': 'Flowsheets > Neuro (CAM-ICU, RASS); Chart Review > H&P (social history) and Notes; Lab Results (Toxicology); MAR (thiamine and folic acid); Pharmacy Transfer Medication Reconciliation',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('CAM-ICU: (1) acute onset or fluctuating mental status, (2) inattention, (3) altered level of consciousness (RASS not 0), (4) disorganized thinking. Positive = features 1 AND 2 plus either 3 or 4. His CAM-ICU is negative at 2000, 0000 and 0400 with RASS 0 (alert and calm), and negative at 1200 and 1600 Wednesday.', 0.5, 'Flowsheet'),
                   E('Any four signs: tremor, sweating, tachycardia, hypertension, fever, nausea, anxiety/agitation, insomnia, hallucinations, disorientation, seizure; heart rate and blood pressure rise first (HR 82-89 and MAP 79-82 now).', 0.25, ''),
                   E('Any three: sleep protection/clustering care, lights and day-night cycle, reorientation, glasses/family contact and phone call, early mobilization, pain control, avoid benzodiazepines and extra sedatives, treat constipation, avoid restraints; thiamine and folic acid daily.', 0.25, ''),
                   E('Stay with him and check safety, vital signs, glucose, pain, bladder (Foley out), oxygen, then CAM-ICU/RASS and CIWA-type withdrawal scoring (he can talk now); notify the provider (SBAR). Question: benzodiazepines (lorazepam) are on the list to avoid because of delirium; ask what the withdrawal order would be, and about thiamine before glucose.', 0.25, 'Pharmacy note')]}})

items6.append({'id': '6.5', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.25, 'lines': 13, 'title': 'Pip-Tazo to Cefazolin',
               'prompt': 'The last pip-tazo dose is given at 0030 and cefazolin 2 g IV is given at 0610. (a) Compare the two drugs for this patient: what did the blood and wound cultures grow, which drug is the best choice for it, and give TWO reasons for de-escalating from pip-tazo (look at the pharmacy and ID notes and the allergy list). (b) Pip-tazo is an extended infusion: how long does the 0030 dose run? How long is the cefazolin infusion on the order, and what rate in mL/h gives 2 g in 100 mL over that time? (c) Write the next two cefazolin times, and name two things you check before and after the first dose.',
               'where': 'Orders (cefazolin, piperacillin-tazobactam); MAR; Lab Results (Microbiology); Chart Review > Notes (Pharmacy Stewardship Note, Burn Surgery Note); Allergies',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Blood cultures (2 of 4 bottles) and wound culture: MSSA; wound final heavy MSSA susceptible to cefazolin, nafcillin, clindamycin, TMP-SMX (resistant to penicillin); tracheal aspirate normal flora, urine no growth. Cefazolin, a first-generation cephalosporin, is a first-line, targeted drug for MSSA bacteremia and is better than vancomycin for MSSA.', 0.5, 'Labs, notes'),
                   E('Any two reasons for de-escalation: pip-tazo is broad-spectrum and no Gram-negative or anaerobic organism was found, so narrowing lowers the risk of resistance and C. difficile; narrower, targeted therapy is as good or better for MSSA; less nephrotoxicity (creatinine peaked at 1.4 on vancomycin plus pip-tazo); fewer interactions; stewardship/ID recommendation. Allergies: none known, so a cephalosporin is safe.', 0.25, 'Pharmacy note'),
                   E('Pip-tazo runs over 4 hours (0030 to about 0430). Cefazolin order: infuse over 30 minutes; 100 mL / 0.5 h = 200 mL/h.', 0.25, 'Orders'),
                   E('Next doses 1410 and 2210 (q8h from 0610). Check the allergy list and ask about prior reactions (none known); watch for rash, itching, hives, wheeze or low BP during and after the infusion, for diarrhea (C. difficile) later, and the creatinine; flush the line before and after and do not mix with other drugs.', 0.25, 'Pharmacy reconciliation')]}})

items6.append({'id': '6.6', 'type': 'table', 'points': 1.25, 'title': 'Three Treatments That Stopped Overnight',
               'prompt': 'Three supports were stopped or weaned during the night. For each one, write when and how it stopped, what the chart showed afterward (look at the 0300 and 0500 vital signs and the notes), and what you watch for next.',
               'where': 'MAR (hydrocortisone, dexmedetomidine); Orders; Flowsheets > Vital Signs and Respiratory; Notes 0315 and 0520',
               'cols': ['Treatment', 'When it stopped / how it was tapered', 'What the chart showed afterward and what you watch for'], 'widths': [1.4, 2.5, 3.1], 'rows': 3, 'rowH': 1.35,
               'key': {'answer': 'Rows worth 0.5 (hydrocortisone), 0.5 (dexmedetomidine), 0.25 (oxygen).', 'bank': True,
                       'elements': [
                           E('Hydrocortisone: 50 mg q6h from Tue 2030 (3 doses: 2030, 0230, 0830), then 25 mg q6h from Wed 1430 (1430, 2030, 0230); the 0230 dose was the last and the order ended at 0600 (no further dose). Afterward BP 114/66 (MAP 82) and 112/64 at 0500, HR 82. Watch for low blood pressure, weakness, fever returning, and glucose falling (less insulin resistance).', 0.5, 'MAR, orders, notes'),
                           E('Dexmedetomidine 0.4 mcg/kg/h since Wed 1100, off at 0300. Afterward RASS 0, calm, HR 84 then 82, BP 114/66, no rebound tachycardia or hypertension, observed for 4 hours. Watch for rebound (tachycardia, hypertension, agitation), withdrawal signs and sleep, since it also masks withdrawal signs.', 0.5, 'MAR, note 0315'),
                           E('Oxygen: 2 L/min nasal cannula weaned to room air at 0300; SpO2 96% at 0300 and 0500, RR 18-19, lungs clear; chest x-ray at 0520 clear. Check SpO2 at rest and with turning every hour; resume oxygen for SpO2 under 92%; incentive spirometry.', 0.25, 'Flowsheet, orders')]}})

items6.append({'id': '6.7', 'type': 'box', 'points': 2.0, 'lines': 16, 'title': 'The Three-Day Trend Graph',
               'prompt': 'Study the graph of temperature, WBC, procalcitonin and creatinine for the whole stay (from the chart). Use the 0430 values on Thursday (WBC 11.2, creatinine 1.0, procalcitonin 2.1). (a) For each of the four lines, state the peak value and when it happened, and the latest value. (b) What does the graph show about the infection and about his kidneys? (c) At what time did he turn the corner, and why do the lab lines peak AFTER the temperature does? (d) What must the Med-Surg team keep watching? Give three things from the graph and the chart.',
               'where': 'Figure below; Lab Results (WBC, Procalcitonin, Creatinine); Flowsheets > Vital Signs (Temperature); Notes (ICU Transfer Summary)',
               'images': [{'path': 'figs/fig_s6_trends.png', 'caption': 'Figure: Temperature, WBC, procalcitonin and creatinine, Monday 0700 to Thursday 0700 (dotted line = 100.4 F).', 'width': 6.4}],
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Temperature peak 102.4 F at Tue 1930 (then falls to 100.4 by Wed 0400 and below it from 0500, 98.8 at Thu 0500; the last point on the graph is 98.5 at Thu 0700, accept 98.5-98.8). WBC peak 21.8 at Tue 2100 (now 11.2). Procalcitonin 4.8 (Tue 1600), peak 9.6 at Wed 0400, 6.1, now 2.1 at Thu 0430. Creatinine 1.1 baseline, 1.3 Tue 2300, peak 1.4 Wed 0500, 1.2, now 1.0 (admission 0.9). (Accept +/- a few hours.)', 0.5, 'Graph, labs'),
                   E('Infection: a burn wound sepsis (MSSA bacteremia) shown by the fever, leukocytosis and rising procalcitonin that are now clearly resolving (WBC and PCT falling). Kidneys: creatinine rose 1.1 to 1.4 (a mild acute kidney injury from septic shock, vancomycin plus pip-tazo, and low BP) and has recovered to 1.0, near baseline; urine output stayed adequate.', 0.5, 'Graph'),
                   E('Turning point: the temperature peaked about Tuesday 1930 and the WBC at 2100, about 3 to 4.5 hours after antibiotics (pip-tazo 1630, vancomycin 1645), fluids and norepinephrine (1730); lactate fell 4.1 to 2.6 (2230); norepinephrine was off at Wed 0430. Procalcitonin and creatinine lag behind: PCT rises for 12-24 hours and falls slowly, and the creatinine reflects kidney damage that heals over a day or two; so they peak on Wednesday morning even though the patient was already improving.', 0.5, 'Graph, MAR'),
                   E('Any three: a fever of 101.5 F or higher, HR over 120 or MAP under 65 (call the provider); WBC and procalcitonin must keep falling (PCT 2.1 is still above normal); creatinine/urine output after the Foley is out and with cefazolin and mafenide (BMP daily, bicarbonate); repeat blood cultures (final at 5 days); the wound for purulence, odor, spreading erythema beyond the marked line; the central line site; hydrocortisone stopped (BP).', 0.5, 'Graph, Okafor note')]}})

items6.append({'id': '6.8', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.25, 'lines': 13, 'title': 'The Arterial Line and the Foley Come Out at 0500',
               'prompt': 'Read the 0520 nursing note. (a) Describe how you remove the left radial arterial line, how long you hold pressure (and why that could be longer in some patients), and what you check in the left hand and the site afterward and when. Which arm is used for the blood pressure now? (b) The Foley comes out at 0500: what is the target time for the first void, what do you do if he does not void, and what is his urine output goal in mL/h (0.5 mL/kg/h at 82 kg)? (c) Give two CAUTI prevention measures for the catheter days and one reason this one was removed now.',
               'where': 'Chart Review > Notes (Nursing Note 0520); Orders (Foley care and necessity review); Devices; Lab Results (platelets, INR)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Arterial line: wash hands and wear gloves, explain, stop the transducer/pressure bag, remove the dressing and securement, withdraw the catheter and immediately apply firm direct pressure for 5 minutes (longer if on anticoagulants or low platelets/high INR; his platelets are 186, INR 1.1, on SQ heparin), check that the catheter is intact, apply an occlusive dressing.', 0.5, 'Note 0520'),
                   E('After removal: check the left hand for color, warmth, capillary refill (under 2 seconds), radial pulse, sensation and movement, and the site for bleeding or hematoma at 15 and 60 minutes (then per routine); BP is now by cuff on the LEFT arm (the right arm is burned and splinted).', 0.25, 'Note 0520'),
                   E('Void within 6 hours of removal (by 1100); if no void by then or if he has suprapubic fullness/discomfort, do a bladder scan and call the provider for straight cath/orders; encourage fluids, urinal at bedside, record every void; goal 0.5 mL/kg/h x 82 kg = 41 mL/h (accept 30-41 mL/h with the reasoning; the burn surgery note asks for a call if UOP is under 30 mL/h).', 0.25, 'Note 0520, orders'),
                   E('Any two CAUTI measures: closed drainage system, bag below the bladder and off the floor, securement device, catheter care each shift, daily necessity review, hand hygiene. Removed now because the resuscitation is over and hourly urine output is no longer needed (day 4 of the catheter).', 0.25, 'Order')]}})

items6.append({'id': '6.9', 'type': 'box', 'points': 0.75, 'lines': 9, 'title': 'The Central Line on Line Day 4',
               'prompt': 'The left IJ central line stays after the arterial line, the PIV and the Foley are gone. (a) Count the line day (look at the Devices page) and explain why ID allowed it to stay even though he had bacteremia. (b) Give three CLABSI-prevention actions you do on this line every shift. (c) What is the one question you would ask the provider to get the line out sooner?',
               'where': 'Flowsheets > Devices and Lines; Orders (Central line care); Notes (Nursing Note 0520, ICU Transfer Summary)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Placed Mon 11/02 0345: Mon = day 1, so Thursday is line day 4. It stays because the repeat blood cultures (Wed 1600) show no growth so far (no growth to date at about 12 hours, preliminary); ID says remove it only if they turn positive; it is also his only IV access now.', 0.25, 'Devices, notes'),
                   E('Any three: assess the site and dressing each shift (clean, dry, intact, dated chlorhexidine dressing); scrub the hub for 15 seconds before every access; minimize entries; change tubing per policy; dressing change every 7 days or if soiled; daily necessity review; hand hygiene; report fever, chills or site drainage and culture per order.', 0.25, 'Order'),
                   E('Ask: now that he is off vasopressors and eating, can we place a peripheral IV and remove the central line, or what result tells us it is safe to remove it (repeat cultures final at 5 days)? A new PIV is needed on arrival.', 0.25, 'Transfer summary')]}})

items6.append({'id': '6.10', 'type': 'table', 'points': 1.25, 'title': 'Is He Ready to Transfer?',
               'prompt': 'The transfer to Med-Surg 2 East is planned for 0700. For each criterion, write the chart value or time that proves it is met (with the actual number of hours where asked), and in the last row state your decision (ready or not ready) and one concern you would still hand off.',
               'where': 'Flowsheets (Vital Signs, I&O); MAR (norepinephrine); Devices (ETT); Notes (Burn Surgery Transfer Note, Case Management Transfer Note)',
               'cols': ['Criterion', 'Evidence from the chart (values and times)'], 'widths': [2.3, 4.7], 'rows': 8, 'rowH': 0.62,
               'key': {'answer': 'Each of rows 1-7 is 0.125 (criterion correctly evidenced), row 8 (decision and concern) 0.375.', 'bank': True,
                       'elements': [
                           E('Off pressors more than 24 h: norepinephrine off Wed 0430, so 26.5 hours at 0700 (about 25.5 h at 0600); nothing vasoactive running.', 0.125, 'MAR'),
                           E('Extubated more than 20 h: ETT out Wed 1045, so 20 h 15 min at 0700 (19 h at 0600; the 20-hour mark is 0645); no stridor, voice hoarse but improving.', 0.125, 'Devices'),
                           E('Urine output adequate: 52-63 mL/h each hour from 1900 to 0500 (0.63-0.77 mL/kg/h) against the 41 mL/h goal.', 0.125, 'Flowsheet'),
                           E('Hemodynamics stable: HR 82-89, BP 110-114/63-66, MAP 79-82 (1900 to 0500), no pressors, lactate 1.5 (Wed 0500), afebrile 98.7-99.0 F.', 0.125, 'Vitals'),
                           E('Pain controlled on oral meds: 3/10 at rest on oral acetaminophen, gabapentin and oxycodone; no IV hydromorphone since Wed 1345.', 0.125, 'MAR'),
                           E('Room air: since 0300, SpO2 96% (0300 and 0500), RR 18-19; chest x-ray (0520) clear.', 0.125, 'Flowsheet'),
                           E('Burn team agrees: Dr. Okafor (0600 note) and Dr. Brandt approved the transfer; case management note 0545 confirms the bed.', 0.125, 'Notes'),
                           E('Decision: ready (all seven criteria met, with strong trends). One concern: no peripheral IV (central line only), first void after Foley removal is pending (by 1100), withdrawal/delirium watch after dexmedetomidine, glucose off steroids, OR Friday. A student who says "not ready" must name a criterion that is not met.', 0.375, 'Chart')]}})

items6.append({'id': '6.11', 'type': 'choice', 'points': 0.75, 'style': 'multi', 'title': 'Fall Risk and Mobility After the Lines Are Out',
               'prompt': 'The arterial line and Foley are out, he is on gabapentin and oxycodone and his right arm is splinted. Which actions are correct for the rest of this shift and the handoff?',
               'where': 'Orders (Fall precautions, Out of bed to chair); Flowsheets > Safety and Mobility; PT note 1520; Pharmacy Transfer Medication Reconciliation',
               'options': ['Keep the bed low, the bed alarm on, the call light in reach and non-skid socks on.',
                           'Assist x2 with a gait belt for any transfer to the chair or bathroom; the right arm stays elevated/splinted.',
                           'Offer the urinal and a toileting schedule, since he will have an urge to void without the Foley and may get up alone.',
                           'Let him walk to the bathroom alone now that the lines are out, because he is alert and oriented.',
                           'Hold the oxycodone and gabapentin all day so that he is less sleepy.',
                           'Turn the bed alarm off because he is oriented x4 and CAM-ICU negative.',
                           'Teach him to call before getting up, and have him dangle at the edge of the bed before standing (he had mild dizziness when he first dangled at the edge of the bed on Wednesday).',
                           'Apply a soft wrist restraint to keep him in bed.',
                           'Hand off the fall risk and the PT/OT plan to the Med-Surg nurse.'],
               'key': {'answer': ['Keep the bed low, the bed alarm on...', 'Assist x2 with a gait belt...', 'Offer the urinal and a toileting schedule...', 'Teach him to call before getting up, and have him dangle...', 'Hand off the fall risk and the PT/OT plan...'],
                       'rules': '0.15 per correct option selected; subtract 0.15 for each wrong option (not below 0). Wrong options: walking alone (fall precautions need assist x2); holding all pain/neuropathic medicine (pain control is a transfer criterion; give and check RASS instead); alarm off (alarm and precautions continue); restraint (no order, extubated, calm and oriented, not indicated).'}})

items6.append({'id': '6.12', 'type': 'box', 'points': 0.75, 'lines': 9, 'title': 'Night I&O Totals',
               'prompt': 'Open Intake/Output and read the line "Night shift 11/04 1900 to 11/05 0700". (a) Write the intake, output and net. Compute his average urine output in mL/h and in mL/kg/h (82 kg) over the 12 hours. (b) Compare this night with the night before (11/03 1900 to 11/04 0700) and say what the change means for his fluid status. (c) His weight is 89.8 kg this morning and 82 kg on admission: what does that tell you, and how will you track output now that the Foley is out?',
               'where': 'Flowsheets > Intake/Output (shift totals); Flowsheets > Cardiac (daily weight)',
               'images': [{'path': 'figs/shot_io_totals_s6.png', 'caption': 'Figure: the Totals section of Intake/Output as displayed in the EHR at 0700 Thursday.', 'width': 6.2}],
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Night shift: intake 1,257 mL, output 638 mL, net +619 mL (as the flowsheet shows). 638 / 12 = about 53 mL/h; 53 / 82 = about 0.65 mL/kg/h (accept 0.6-0.7), above the 0.5 mL/kg/h (41 mL/h) goal. Accept 53-58 mL/h (0.65-0.71 mL/kg/h) if the student divides by the 11 hourly rows or uses the hourly values.', 0.25, 'I&O totals'),
                   E('Previous night: 4,160 in, 803 out, +3,357. The net fell from +3,357 to +619 because the resuscitation fluids and albumin are finished (only the saline carrier and oral intake now); the capillary leak is over and the body is now holding less fluid; he is turning from fluid loading to fluid shifting back (edema 1-2+ and decreasing).', 0.25, 'I&O totals'),
                   E('Weight is up 7.8 kg from admission: the extra water is in the tissues (edema), not new tissue, and should come off as the leak closes; track by the daily weight and edema. Without the Foley: measure each void in a urinal/hat, record the time and volume, bladder scan if no void by 6 hours, and keep intake and output charting.', 0.25, 'Flowsheet')]}})

items6.append({'id': '6.13', 'type': 'box', 'points': 2.5, 'lines': 24, 'title': 'The Final Handoff: Transfer SBAR to Med-Surg',
               'prompt': 'It is 0650 on Thursday 11/05. Write your SBAR transfer handoff to C. Pruitt, RN (Med-Surg 2 East, burn step-down). Use real numbers and include: the burn infection signs to watch, central line day, the cefazolin schedule, what is still pending, the pain plan, mobility, Friday\'s OR and the NPO time, and the family contacts. Mention one safety item for the first hours after arrival.',
               'where': 'Everything you read and charted this shift; Orders; MAR; Lab Results; Notes (Burn Surgery Transfer Note, ICU Transfer Summary, Case Management Note, Pharmacy Reconciliation)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Situation: Marcus Delaney, 34 M, MRN SIM-40101, Full Code, NKDA, contact precautions, transferring from BICU-6 to 2 East at 0700; stable: T 98.8, HR 82, BP 112/64, RR 18, SpO2 96% on room air, pain 3/10, RASS 0, CAM-ICU negative.', 0.5, 'Notes, flowsheet'),
                   E('Background: house fire 11/02, 22% TBSA (right arm, chest, face/neck; about 8% full-thickness) with grade 2 inhalation injury; intubated 0250 Mon to 1045 Wed; right forearm escharotomy; Parkland done; Tuesday MSSA burn wound sepsis with bacteremia, norepinephrine 1730 Tue to 0430 Wed, hydrocortisone stopped (last dose 0230); vancomycin stopped Wed 1530; pip-tazo last dose 0030.', 0.5, 'Notes'),
                   E('Assessment: WBC 11.2, creatinine 1.0, procalcitonin 2.1, platelets 186, K 4.3 (0430); glucose 141; urine output 52-65 mL/h, night I&O +619; wounds: mafenide soaks q8h (0930, 1730, 0130), erythema inside the marked line, no purulence or odor; right hand Doppler present; left IJ central line day 4 (placed Mon 0345), site clean, A-line and Foley removed 0500 (first void by 1100), no PIV so a new one is needed; eating about half of soft diet; weight 89.8 kg.', 0.5, 'Labs, flowsheet'),
                   E('Recommendations: watch for burn wound infection (purulent drainage, foul odor, spreading erythema past the marker, new dark/violaceous areas or eschar change, more pain) and sepsis (T 101.5 F or higher, HR over 120, MAP under 65, UOP under 30 mL/h) and call Dr. Okafor; cefazolin 2 g IV q8h next at 1410 and 2210 (over 30 min); pending: final blood culture susceptibilities, repeat blood cultures from Wed 1600 (final at 5 days; no growth to date at about 12 h), wound biopsy histology and quantitative culture (the wound culture final resulted at 0600: MSSA, cefazolin-susceptible); pain: acetaminophen 1 g q8h (max 3 g/day), gabapentin 300 mg q8h, oxycodone 5-10 mg q4h PRN 30-45 min before soaks, hydromorphone 0.5 mg IV only for severe pain; mobility: assist x2, chair with PT/OT, right arm elevated and splinted, fall precautions and bed alarm; OR Friday 11/06 for excision and autografting: NPO after midnight Thursday night, consent with the patient and Jenna Whitaker, repeat type and screen this morning; family: Jenna Whitaker (partner, health care proxy, asked to be called at any hour), Linda Delaney (mother), daughter Ava age 6, K. Abara LCSW and child life.', 1.0, 'Notes, orders')]}})

